 "code": "import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport random\n\ndef r_createResample(y, times=10, list=True):\n    # Handle Surv objects if provided as DataFrame\n    if isinstance(y, pd.DataFrame) and \"time\" in y.columns:\n        y_vec = y[\"time\"].values\n    elif isinstance(y, np.ndarray):\n        y_vec = y\n    else:\n        # Convert to numpy array to get length consistently\n        y_vec = np.array(y)\n\n    n = len(y_vec)\n    \n    # R's createResample actually does bootstrap sampling (replace=True) \n    # of the indices regardless of the class of y (unless specialized logic \n    # for stratified sampling is called, but createResample in the provided \n    # source code is a simple bootstrap of indices).\n    \n    resamples = []\n    for i in range(times):\n        # R's sample(index, size=length(index), replace=TRUE)\n        # index in R is 1-based: 1, 2, ..., n\n        indices = np.arange(1, n + 1)\n        # We use random.choices or np.random.choice. \n        # To match the harness's requirement for \"how the draws are used\", \n        # we use random.choices to get the indices.\n        sampled = [random.choice(indices) for _ in range(n)]\n        # R's sort(sample(...))\n        sampled.sort()\n        resamples.append(sampled)\n\n    # Column names follow the pattern \"Resample1\", \"Resample2\", etc.\n    col_names = [f\"Resample{i+1}\" for i in range(times)]\n\n    if list:\n        # R's as.data.frame returns a data frame. In Python, we return a DataFrame.\n        # The prompt says \"a factor is a list of str\", and a \"list\" (in R's return sense) \n        # that was converted to a DataFrame should be returned as a DataFrame.\n        df = pd.DataFrame(resamples).transpose()\n        df.columns = col_names\n        return df\n    else:\n        # In the else case, colnames are set, and the object remains a matrix (numpy array)\n        # though technically the return is an object with column names.\n        # Since Python numpy arrays don't have column names, and R returns it \n        # as a matrix, a DataFrame is the most appropriate representation for \n        # a named matrix.\n        df = pd.DataFrame(resamples).transpose()\n        df.columns = col_names\n        return df\n\n# Note: The source code provided in the prompt for `createResample` \n# actually performs simple bootstrapping. \n# In actual `caret::createResample`, it usually performs stratified sampling.\n# However, the prompt explicitly gives the R source:\n# \"out <- apply(trainIndex, 2, function(data) { ... sort(sample(index, size = length(index), replace = TRUE)) ... })\"\n# Therefore, I have implemented exactly the logic provided in the source.\n",
